$DIR/main.p:68:10: cannot pass &arr to elem, which keeps it after returning
    &arr is a pointer to arr. arr is a local array of Element (declared at
    main.p:67). elem passes p to call (main.p:38), which passes s to s.String
    (which may call main.(*S).String) (main.p:32), which stores s in package
    variable keepS (main.p:17), so &arr would outlive arr.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If elem should keep its own copy of arr, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[[2]S] from new, a shared[[2]S],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

